description:
**What:** RFC 8620 §5.3 patch paths use JSON Pointer /-separated paths. The current copy handler in card.rs does naive m.insert(k, v) which treats a key like 'name/full' as a literal top-level key (corrupting the data). Oracle: RFC 8620 §5.3, §5.4.

**Algorithm:**
1. Replace the naive insert loop in the copy handler with a JSON Pointer walk-and-set function:
   fn apply_patch(card_val: &mut Value, path: &str, val: Value) {
       // split on '/', decode ~0 as '~' and ~1 as '/' per RFC 6901
       // walk into nested objects, creating intermediate maps as needed
       // if val is Value::Null, remove the key (RFC 8620 §5.3 null = delete)
       // otherwise set the value at the leaf
   }
2. Apply this to each key-value pair in the copy spec object.
3. Tests:
   - copy_with_flat_patch_applies_correctly: spec object {"id":"card1","addressBookIds":{"ab2":true}} → copied card has addressBookIds overridden. Oracle: RFC 8620 §5.4.
   - copy_with_nested_path_patch: spec object {"id":"card1","name/full":"Alice Smith"} → card.name.full=="Alice Smith" with no literal key "name/full" at top level. Oracle: RFC 8620 §5.3.
   - copy_with_null_removes_property: spec {"id":"card1","emails/work":null} → card.emails.work removed. Oracle: RFC 8620 §5.3 null means delete.
   - copy_source_not_found: returns notCopied with type notFound.
4. cargo test -p jmap-contacts-server.

**Acceptance criteria:**
- name/full patch correctly sets nested property.
- null patch removes nested property.
- cargo test -p jmap-contacts-server exits 0.
